--expect
0/0 1/1 97/97 65/65 223/223 1000/1000 50,102 -1,-1 -1,-1
--expect-why
DERIVED BY HAND from `casemap.kin` and this fixture's table, which is ALL
ZEROS. The driver's own comment says why: the probe checks that three targets
generated the SAME SEARCH over a table they index the same way, not that the
mapping is right -- the real table is checked against Clojure by the language
suite. So every number below comes from running the two searches on paper
against zeros, and NONE of them is a Unicode fact.

THE ROW COUNTS ARE REAL even though the contents are not, and they have to
be: `case-ranges-n` and `case-full-n` are count accessors that inline as
literals, so the generated search carries the SHIPPED table's row counts --
currently 408 and 104 (Unicode 18.0.0; `bin/casetable`'s own header) -- no
matter what this fixture's own array capacity is. `CASE_UPPER_LEN` and
`CASE_FULL_UPPER_LEN`, below and in the search math that follows, are NOT
that: they are this fixture's own local constants (200, 102), fixed on
purpose, and everything after this paragraph is about THOSE two, not about
the row counts above.

  case-ranges-n inlines 408 (real row count); CASE_UPPER_LEN = 200 (fixture)
  case-full-n inlines 104 (real row count); CASE_FULL_UPPER_LEN = 102 (fixture)

`case-map` OVER ZEROS -- every code point maps to ITSELF, for two different
reasons, and the split is at zero.

  Each probed row gives s = 0, e = 0, delta = 0, stride = 0.

  cp > 0 (1, 97, 65, 223, 1000): `c > e` is true at every midpoint, so `lo`
     walks past `hi`, the `forever` breaks, and the function falls through to
     its last line: `return cp`. That is the "a code point with no entry maps
     to itself" arm, reached by EXHAUSTING the search.
  cp = 0: `c < s` and `c > e` are both false, so 0 is IN RANGE [0,0]. Stride
     is 0, not 1, so the stride arm runs: `(c - s) & 1` = 0, which is even, so
     it returns `c + delta` = 0 + 0 = 0. Reached by FINDING a row.

Both print the input back, so `0/0 1/1 97/97 65/65 223/223 1000/1000` -- and
the two paths differing matters: cp = 0 is the one input in this list that
exercises the hit branch, the delta addition and the stride test at all. Note
`97/97` and `65/65` would be `65/97` and `65/97` against the real table; the
zeros are what make them identities here.

`full-index` OVER ZEROS -- and this is where the search's exact shape shows.

  `50,102` for cp = 0. Zero is a HIT, and the index returned is wherever the
  binary search first lands:
    up:   base2 = 0, lo = 0, hi = CASE_FULL_UPPER_LEN - 1 = 101.
          mid = lo + (hi - lo)/2 = 0 + 101/2 = 50. Row 50's first field is 0,
          equal to c, so it returns base2 + mid = 50 on the FIRST probe.
    down: base2 = CASE_FULL_UPPER_LEN = 102 (fixture), hi = (104 - 102) - 1 = 1,
          where 104 is `case-full-n`'s REAL inlined count -- a section of TWO
          rows in THIS fixture's search, not the real table's actual one-row
          lowercase section (`casemap.kin`'s own doc now says "103 of them
          upward... exactly one downward", about the real 104-row table; this
          fixture's CASE_FULL_UPPER_LEN is a fixed local constant and is not
          meant to track that any more, which is the whole point of pinning
          the array capacity separately from it). mid = 0 + (1 - 0)/2 = 0 --
          still the FIRST probed index regardless of hi being 0 or 1 -- hit,
          returns 102 + 0 = 102.
  `50` therefore pins the midpoint formula: `lo + (hi - lo)/2` on [0,101] with
  integer division truncating toward zero. `102` pins the other thing the doc
  insists on -- the index is ABSOLUTE, not section-relative. A search that
  answered `mid` instead of `base2 + mid` would print `50,0` here, and a
  caller would then read the uppercase row 0 for a lowercase hit.

  `-1,-1` twice, for 223 and 1000. Both are greater than every probed 0, so
  `lo` climbs past `hi` and the search falls out to `return -1`. -1 and not 0:
  0 is a VALID index into this table (it is sharp-s's own row in the real
  data), so a miss reported as 0 would be read as a hit on the first entry.
  223 is chosen precisely because it IS sharp s -- against the shipped table
  it would be a hit at 0, and here it must be a miss, which is the clearest
  statement that this probe is about the search and not the data.
